---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "flavcity protein powder",
      "flav city protein powder",
      "flavcity whey protein",
      "flavcity protein review"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Clean Protein Picks",
    link: "/best-clean-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker="Brand Review" badge="Label-Checked" author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">FlavCity Protein Powder Review</Fragment>
      FlavCity's Protein Smoothies, read off the brand's own product pages: what is in a serving, how much of the protein is collagen, what the plant-based version uses, and how the price compares with plain grass-fed whey.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#verdict">The Short Verdict</a>
      <a href="#the-brand">Who Makes FlavCity</a>
      <a href="#whats-inside">What Is Inside a Scoop</a>
      <a href="#collagen-question">The Collagen Question</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Our Top Picks</a>
      <a href="#range-compared">Flavors, Formats and the Plant Version</a>
      <a href="#cost-per-25g">Cost per 25 g of Protein</a>
      <a href="#testing">Testing and the Prop 65 Warning</a>
      <a href="#who-should-skip">Who Should Skip It</a>
      <a href="#how-to-use">Where to Buy and How to Use It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          FlavCity sells protein the way a food brand would: as a flavored smoothie mix with grass-fed whey, collagen, banana, coconut milk and functional mushrooms, sweetened with stevia and no added sugar. The brand lists about 25 g of protein and 10 g of collagen per serving. It is a well-made, real-ingredient product for people who want a ready-to-shake breakfast drink. It is also expensive per gram of protein, it does not publish third-party testing, it carries a Prop 65 warning, and we could not confirm an official listing on Amazon US. For most people, a plain grass-fed whey plus a banana gets you most of the way for far less.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Verdict
          </h2>

          <p>
            FlavCity's Protein Smoothies are a good product built for a specific job: a flavored, ready-to-shake breakfast or snack drink made from recognizable ingredients. The brand lists about 25 g of protein and 10 g of grass-fed collagen per serving, with grass-fed whey concentrate, milk protein, coconut milk powder, banana powder, stevia and two functional mushrooms in the chocolate flavor, and 0 g of added sugar.
          </p>

          <p>
            As a protein powder judged on protein, it is weaker. The product page does not say whether the 10 g of collagen counts toward the 25 g, and collagen is an incomplete protein that does little for muscle compared with whey. If it does count, each serving carries closer to 15 g of high-quality protein. Either way, you are paying for flavor, fruit and extras as much as for protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            Price is the other sticking point. At the one-time prices on FlavCity's site, a serving costs about $3 to $4, roughly double the cost per 25 g of a plain grass-fed whey such as Naked Whey. A subscription trims about 15%. Availability is patchy too: several flavors were listed as sold out when we checked, and we could not confirm an official FlavCity listing on Amazon US.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our verdict: buy FlavCity if you value a real-ingredient, stevia-sweetened smoothie mix and the price does not bother you, and you are happy to buy direct. Skip it if you want the most protein for your money, need third-party testing, or want to buy on Amazon. A one-ingredient grass-fed whey blended with fruit gives you a similar drink for far less, and our <a href="/best-clean-protein-powder/">best clean protein powder</a> ranking lists the strongest options.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE ONE-LINE ANSWER</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A nice smoothie mix, an expensive protein powder</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">About 25 g of protein with 10 g of collagen per serving, at roughly $3 to $4 a serving. Buy plain grass-fed whey and add your own fruit if protein per dollar matters.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="the-brand" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Who Makes FlavCity
          </h2>

          <p>
            FlavCity began as a food and cooking media brand, best known for Bobby Parrish's YouTube channel, which the brand still links from its site. The channel built a large audience reviewing grocery products and reading ingredient labels, and the product line grew out of that: protein smoothies, coffee and other pantry items sold under the FlavCity name. The company behind the trademark is listed on the site as DTBE, LLC.
          </p>

          <p>
            That background shapes the product. The pitch on FlavCity's site is flavor first and real food always: recognizable ingredients, real fruit, grass-fed whey, no artificial flavors, no GMOs and, in several products, no added sugar. That is a food-label philosophy rather than a sports-nutrition one, and it explains why the product reads like a smoothie recipe rather than a gym tub.
          </p>

          <p>
            It also explains what is missing. Sports-nutrition brands tend to compete on protein per scoop, certifications and price per gram. FlavCity competes on ingredient quality and taste, and its pages make no third-party testing claims at all. For a clean-label brand, published lab results would be a natural next step, and some competitors in the same space already publish them.
          </p>

          <p>
            FlavCity sells primarily through its own online store, with subscribe-and-save pricing and free shipping on larger or subscription orders. We could not confirm an official listing on Amazon US at the time of writing, so any FlavCity product you find there may come through a third-party seller. If you want to buy on Amazon, the alternatives below are the practical route.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="whats-inside" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Is Inside a Scoop
          </h2>

          <p>
            The chocolate Protein Smoothie's key ingredients, as listed on FlavCity's product page, are grass-fed undenatured whey protein concentrate, grass-fed collagen, organic cocoa powder, coconut milk powder, grass-fed milk protein concentrate, fermented nutritional yeast protein, banana powder, unrefined sea salt, organic stevia leaf extract and stevia Reb M, and organic cordyceps and reishi mushroom powders. It contains milk.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Ingredient</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What it does</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Our take</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Grass-fed whey concentrate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Main protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High quality, complete protein</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Grass-fed collagen</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 10 g per serving</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Incomplete protein, low in leucine</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Milk protein concentrate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Extra dairy protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Complete, slower digesting</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Yeast protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Plant-based protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Small share, complete protein</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Coconut milk, banana</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Creaminess, flavor</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Adds some calories and fat</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Stevia Reb A and Reb M</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sweetness</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No added sugar</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Reishi, cordyceps</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Functional mushrooms</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Weak evidence at these doses</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The protein base is genuinely good. Grass-fed whey concentrate and milk protein concentrate are both complete dairy proteins with plenty of leucine, and whey concentrate is what most of the clean-label category uses. Undenatured is a label term for whey processed at lower temperatures; it is a reasonable quality marker, though the evidence that it matters for muscle is limited.
          </p>

          <p>
            The extras are where we would be more skeptical. FlavCity describes reishi and cordyceps as functional mushrooms shown to increase focus and cognition. The human research on both is small and mixed, and a protein smoothie does not list how much of each it contains, so treat them as a flavor of the brand's philosophy rather than a reason to buy. Coconut milk and banana powder add creaminess and taste, at the cost of some calories.
          </p>

          <p>
            The sweetener choice is a clear plus for many buyers: stevia, in two forms, with no sucralose, no sugar alcohols and 0 g of added sugar per the brand. The calorie count is not in the product page text, only in the nutrition panel image, so check the pouch or packet. Expect more calories than a plain whey because of the coconut milk and banana.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="collagen-question" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Collagen Question
          </h2>

          <p>
            FlavCity's pages describe the smoothies as having 25 g of protein and 10 g of collagen. What they do not spell out in text is whether those numbers overlap. On a nutrition panel, collagen counts as protein, so if the 10 g is inside the 25 g, the serving carries about 15 g of whey, milk and yeast protein plus 10 g of collagen. That matters a lot for what the shake does.
          </p>

          <p>
            Collagen is an incomplete protein. It lacks tryptophan and is low in leucine, the amino acid most strongly linked to triggering muscle protein synthesis. Studies on post-workout recovery mostly use 20 to 25 g of a complete protein such as whey, which delivers roughly 2.5 to 3 g of leucine. Fifteen grams of whey and milk protein falls short of that on its own.
          </p>

          <p>
