        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Testing and the Prop 65 Warning
          </h2>

          <p>
            FlavCity's product pages make no third-party testing claims. We found no NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport or Clean Label Project claim on the pages we read, and no published certificates of analysis for heavy metals. For a brand whose pitch is ingredient transparency, that is the biggest gap, and the reason we cannot rank it highly in our clean protein guide.
          </p>

          <p>
            The site does say, plainly, that some FlavCity products carry a California Prop 65 warning due to naturally occurring lead and cadmium in certain ingredients, and the chocolate and plant-based product pages we read include a Prop 65 notice. That honesty is worth noting. Cocoa, plant proteins and some superfood powders tend to carry more lead and cadmium than dairy proteins, because these metals come from soil.
          </p>

          <p>
            A Prop 65 warning is not a measured result. California's thresholds are very low, and products can carry a warning without exceeding any federal limit. But without published lab numbers, you have no way to know how far above or below those thresholds a given flavor sits. Brands that publish batch results let you check; FlavCity, at the time of writing, does not.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our advice: if heavy metals are a concern, favor the non-chocolate dairy flavors over cocoa or plant-based ones, rotate protein sources, and ask the brand for lab results. Pregnant readers and anyone buying for children should talk to a doctor or registered dietitian before using any protein product daily. Our guide to <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">whether protein powder is safe</a> explains how to read Prop 65 warnings and lab reports.
          </p>
        </div>

